.TH MCH 1 "2026-07-04" "meshherder 0.20.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mch \- manage MeshCore repeater nodes through the mchd daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mch
[\fB--socket\fR \fIPATH\fR]
\fIGROUP\fR \fICOMMAND\fR
[\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mch
is the command line client for Mesh Herder. It talks to
.BR mchd (8)
over a Unix socket and shows fleet status, validates configuration,
triggers syncs, runs carefully checked one-off commands, and guides node
commissioning.
.PP
Commands are organised under noun groups so the top-level menu stays
shallow:
.B service
(process lifecycle and config validation),
.B fleet
(whole-fleet status, issues, sync, plan, logs, suggest),
.B node
(per-node inventory, lifecycle, and diagnostics),
.B config
(desired state plus backup), and
.B gateway
(operate the locally attached gateway node). Run
.B "mch \-\-help"
to list the groups and, for example,
.B "mch node \-\-help"
to list a group's commands.
.PP
Mesh Herder treats the SQLite database as the authoritative source of desired
state and observed state alike. The daemon applies only the settings that are
explicitly configured for enforcement; the default setting policy is audit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the installed version and exit.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-socket \fIPATH\fR
Path to the
.B mchd
Unix socket. Defaults to
.IR /run/meshherder/mchd.sock .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show command help.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS shortcuts and help
.TP
.B status \fR[\fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR]
Shortcut for
.BR "fleet status" .
.TP
.B sync \fR[\fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR]
Shortcut for
.BR "fleet sync" .
.TP
.B help selectors
Explain how to name a node on the command line (label, pubkey prefix, or mesh name).
.TP
.B help concepts
Explain desired vs reported values, policy (enforce/audit/fact/skip), scope
precedence, what a sync cycle does, and the exit codes.
.TP
.B help states
Explain what each node STATE means (configured, partial, unreachable, offline,
and the rest) and how TRIES relates to unreachable nodes.
.SS daemon \(em process lifecycle
.TP
.B service start
Start
.B mchd
through systemd.
.TP
.B service stop
Stop
.B mchd
through systemd.
.TP
.B service restart
Restart
.B mchd
through systemd.
.TP
.B service status
Show the service status (a mirror of
.BR "systemctl status mchd" ).
.TP
.B service logs \fR[\-n \fILINES\fR] [\-f]
Show the daemon process journal (startup, crashes, tracebacks) from
.BR journalctl ,
distinct from
.B "fleet logs"
(per-node activity).
.TP
.B service configtest \fR[\-\-config \fIPATH\fR]
Validate the daemon's
.I config.yaml
(and secrets if readable) without contacting
.BR mchd .
Unrecognised fact names are reported as warnings with close-match suggestions.
.SS fleet \(em whole-fleet views
.TP
.B fleet status
Show the fleet table, including state, label, group, firmware, neighbour count,
and last seen time. Rows are sorted by group then label. Below the table it
prints a short issue summary (drift, problem-state, and open-node counts) and,
when most of the fleet is down, a gateway advisory; the full issue tables live in
.BR "fleet issues" .
.TP
.B fleet status \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Show the fleet table filtered to matching nodes. A single match prints a hint
pointing at
.B "node show"
for the full detail view. Add
.B \-\-json
to emit the report model for scripting.
.TP
.B fleet sync
Start an immediate full sync cycle in the background. Rate limiting still
applies.
.TP
.B fleet sync \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Sync one node immediately while streaming fact reads, setting checks, enforcement
actions, operations, and timeout failures as they occur.
.TP
.B fleet issues \fR[\fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR]
Show only the fleet issue sections (drifted settings, problem states, open
nodes). Exits 0 when clean and 2 when any issue is present, for cron alerting.
.TP
.B fleet suggest \fR[\fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR]
Show suggested settings based on each node's neighbour count. With a match, shows
detailed suggestions and the ready-to-run
.B "mch node set"
commands to apply them.
.TP
.B fleet plan
Show desired-versus-known changes without sending mesh commands or writing to
nodes.
.TP
.B fleet plan \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Filter planned writes, audit drift, and unknown values.
.TP
.B fleet logs
Show recent per-node activity across all nodes.
.TP
.B fleet logs \fIPREFIX\fR
Show recent activity for one node.
.SS node \(em per-node inventory, lifecycle, and diagnostics
.TP
.B node new
Interactively add a fresh or replacement MeshCore node.
.TP
.B node status \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Show full detail for one node: facts, all desired settings, schedule, and recent
activity. This is the detail view (formerly reached by a single
.B "fleet status"
match); add
.B \-\-json
to emit the node report for scripting.
.TP
.B node move \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \fIGROUP\fR
Move the node into
.I GROUP
(which must already exist; create it with
.BR "mch group create" ).
Pass
.B \-
as
.I GROUP
to ungroup the node. Inventory metadata only; run
.B "fleet sync"
to reconverge under the new group's settings.
.TP
.B node label \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \fR[\fITEXT\fR]
Set the node's host-only label, or clear it when
.I TEXT
is omitted. Labels may use only letters, digits, dashes, underscores, and dots.
.TP
.B node discover \-\-radio \fIFREQ,BW,SF,CR\fR [\-\-dwell \fISECONDS\fR] [\-\-unknown\-only]
Temporarily switch the gateway radio, send an active MeshCore discovery request,
collect responses for a bounded dwell period, then restore the gateway radio.
This prints discovered public keys, type, SNR, status, and matching label. It
does not add DB nodes, gateway contacts, secrets, or config entries. With
.B \-\-unknown\-only ,
fully managed nodes are hidden while config-only, DB-only, and unmanaged nodes
remain visible.
.TP
.B node adopt \fIPUBKEY\fR \-\-radio \fIFREQ,BW,SF,CR\fR [\-\-label \fILABEL\fR]
Adopt an already-running discovered repeater by full 64-character public key.
This adds/updates the local DB node, adds and favourites a gateway contact,
installs a direct (zero-hop) route on it, then sends one directed
.B ver
on the discovered radio to confirm the route carries a command (restoring the
gateway radio afterwards). It does not edit
.IR config.yaml ,
.IR secrets.yaml ,
or hardware settings.
.TP
.B node retire \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Stop managing a node: set its state to
.BR retired ,
unfavourite its gateway contact (clearing the eviction pin; the contact itself is
kept), and exclude it from sync, while keeping its history. Retire-don't-delete is
the normal way to decommission a node.
.TP
.B node unretire \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Return a retired node to management: re-favourite it and queue it to reconverge.
.TP
.B node prune \fR[\fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR]
Preview deleting retired nodes. With no argument this lists every retired node;
with a
.I LABEL_OR_PREFIX
it targets that one. A still-managed (non-retired) node is refused
\(em retire it first, because an un-retired node is re-seeded from gateway
favourites on the next restart.
.TP
.B node prune \fR[\fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR] \-\-yes
Hard-delete the previewed retired node(s): local facts, settings, operations,
logs, and the node row. This does not edit
.IR secrets.yaml .
.TP
.B node clean facts \fINODE\fR [\-\-fact \fIKEY\fR] [\-\-yes]
List or delete cached fact rows that are no longer present in the configured
.B facts:
list. Scope may be one node,
.B \-\-group
.IR NAME ,
or
.B \-\-all .
Without
.B \-\-yes
this is a dry run.
.TP
.B node clean settings \fINODE\fR [\-\-setting \fIKEY\fR] [\-\-yes]
List or delete observed setting rows whose key is no longer in the node's resolved
desired set (config plus database). Scope may be one node,
.B \-\-group
.IR NAME ,
or
.B \-\-all .
Without
.B \-\-yes
this is a dry run. Observed rows are a cache and are re-read on the next sync if
still wanted.
.TP
.B node path \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR
Show the gateway's cached management route metadata for one node without probing
the mesh. Routes may be
.BR direct ,
.BR stored ,
.BR flood ,
or
.BR missing .
.B flood
and
.B missing
are marked
.B "path repair needed"
because full management syncs avoid flood-routed contacts.
.TP
.B node path \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \-\-discover
Ask MeshCore for a fresh path-discovery candidate and print the value to review.
The gateway contact is not changed.
.TP
.B node path \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \-\-advert
Print the gateway's cached advert path candidate without changing the contact.
.TP
.B node path \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \-\-apply \fIPATH[:MODE]\fR
Apply an operator-approved replacement path to the gateway contact. Re-run
.B "fleet sync"
for the node after applying. A wrong path can make management less reachable
until repaired again.
.TP
.B node probe \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \-\-radio \fIFREQ,BW,SF,CR\fR [\-\-login] [\-\-advert]
Read-only reachability check: temporarily switch the gateway radio, send one
directed
.B ver
to the node, restore the radio, and report the result.
.B \-\-login
authenticates with the node's configured password first (an auth-required node
silently drops unauthenticated commands, so a bare
.B ver
times out).
.B \-\-advert
sends a gateway zero-hop advert on the radio first so the node can learn a route
back. Changes nothing on the node, the contact route,
.IR config.yaml ,
or
.IR secrets.yaml .
.TP
.B node exec \fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR \fICOMMAND...\fR
Run a raw MeshCore CLI command on a managed node. Dangerous commands are blocked
or require confirmation.
.SS gateway \(em operate the locally attached gateway node
.TP
.B gateway contacts \fR[\fILABEL_OR_PREFIX\fR]
Show repeater contacts visible to the gateway that are not yet managed. Without a
filter it prints a compact table (the gateway can see hundreds of contacts). When
a filter matches only a handful, each is shown as a ready-to-run
.B "mch node adopt"
command plus its
.I secrets.yaml
snippet; a larger match stays a table. Add
.B \-\-json
to emit the contact list for scripting.
.TP
.B gateway admin \fR[\fICLI ARGS...\fR]
Stop
.BR mchd ,
run
.B meshcore-cli
against the configured gateway serial device, then restart
.B mchd
after the admin session.
.SS config \(em desired state and backup
.TP
.B node set \fINODE KEY\fR [\fIVALUE\fR] [\-\-policy \fIenforce|audit|fact|skip\fR]
Record a desired setting on one node in the database, the authoritative source
of desired state. \fIKEY\fR is a writable setting (for example \fBname\fR or
\fBtxdelay\fR) or a directive (\fBname_prefix\fR, \fBdefault_policy\fR,
\fBvia_radio\fR). The write is synchronous and does not trigger a sync; run
.B "fleet sync"
.I NODE
to converge. For wider scopes use
.B "group set"
.I "NAME KEY"
or
.B "fleet set"
.IR KEY .
.TP
.B node unset \fINODE KEY\fR
Remove a node's database-set desired setting; observed state is left intact.
The scope-matching verbs
.B "group unset"
.I "NAME KEY"
and
.B "fleet unset"
.I KEY
clear group and fleet defaults.
.TP
.B config show \fINODE\fR [\fIKEY\fR]
Show desired state. With just
.I NODE
it lists every key that resolves for the node (policy, desired, which scope it
came from, and the observed value). With a
.I KEY
it traces that one key: how each scope sets it, what resolves, and what is
observed. Accepts
.B \-\-group
.I NAME
and
.B \-\-global
to list the rows stored at a group's or the global scope instead (no
.I NODE
in that form).
.TP
.B fleet import [\fIFILE\fR] [\-\-yes]
Seed the database's desired state and node label/group inventory from
.IR config.yaml ,
or from an explicit
.I FILE
(a legacy or exported config), flattening settings and directives at node, group,
and global scope into desired rows. The explicit-file form lets the migration
on-ramp work against a daemon that no longer keeps desired state in its own
.IR config.yaml .
Without
.B \-\-yes
this is a dry-run preview. Re-running is idempotent. A node identified by a full
64-hex public key (as
.B "config export"
emits) is created even if the gateway has not seen it yet \(em the restore path; a
node given only a short prefix needs to be known already and is otherwise skipped
with a warning. If the file contains retired nodes (tagged
.BR "retired: true" ),
choose
.B \-\-retired keep
to restore them as retired or
.B \-\-retired skip
to omit them; a write is refused until one is chosen.
.TP
.B fleet export
Print the whole fleet as config.yaml-shaped YAML on standard output \(em a complete
backup and the inverse of
.BR "config import" .
Includes every node's full public key, label, group, desired settings, and
operations (even a node with no desired settings); retired nodes are tagged
.BR "retired: true" .
Redirect to save, for example
.BR "mch fleet export > backup.yaml" ,
and rebuild after a server or gateway loss with
.BR "mch fleet import backup.yaml \-\-yes" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/meshherder/config.yaml
Daemon and gateway settings (desired state now lives in the database).
.TP
.I /etc/meshherder/secrets.yaml
Node passwords and private keys. Usually mode 600 and readable only by the
service account.
.TP
.I /var/lib/meshherder/mch.db
Desired and observed state, command history, facts, and sync schedule.
.TP
.I /run/meshherder/mchd.sock
Default daemon socket.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error (bad usage, daemon unreachable, or a command that failed).
.TP
.B 2
Issues present. Returned only by
.BR "fleet issues" ,
so a cron job can alert on fleet health
.RB ( "mch fleet issues >/dev/null || notify" ).
.B "fleet status"
is a viewing command and always exits 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B mch service configtest
Validate the active config file, and validate secrets too if they are readable.
.TP
.B sudo mch service configtest
Validate both production config and mode-600 secrets.
.TP
.B mch fleet status qh
Show nodes matching
.IR qh .
.TP
.B mch fleet sync qh-wold
Sync one node immediately and show live progress while waiting for mesh replies.
.TP
.B mch node prune old-site --yes
Hard-delete one retired node and its local history.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mchd (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
